<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stamford, Lincolnshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stamford is a market town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-welland">River Welland</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> it was one of the Five Boroughs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, submitting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> in 918. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it prospered from the wool and cloth trade and had many churches and religious houses, and its stone buildings survive largely intact. Burghley House, home of the Cecil family, stands just outside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stamford-lincolnshire.jpg" alt="Stamford, Lincolnshire" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Harrop · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barn_Hill,_Stamford_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7627373.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Stamford forms a south-westerly protrusion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> between Rutland to the north and west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> to the south and Northamptonshire to the south-west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-welland">River Welland</a> was historically the boundary between Lincolnshire and Northamptonshire, and the land south of it was Stamford Baron in Northamptonshire. Much of the town is built on Middle Jurassic Lincolnshire limestone, and the area is known for its limestone and slate quarries. Cream-coloured Collyweston stone slate covers the roofs of many of its stone buildings. The name means stony ford and appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> as Steanford in 922 and Stanford in 942.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermine-street">Ermine Street</a> across what is now Burghley Park and forded the Welland to the west of the town. Their own town lay to the north at Great Casterton, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> later chose Stamford as the main town because it stood on a larger river. By the early 1500s the wool and broadcloth industry on which Stamford depended had declined sharply, and in 1548 the town, reduced to about 800 people, merged its eleven parishes into six. After almost 150 years of stagnation the population began to rise again in the second half of the 17th century, with leather and fibre working, wood and stone working as the main activities.</p><p>For over 600 years, until 1839, Stamford held a bull run each year on 13 November, St Brice's day. Local tradition says William de Warenne, 5th Earl of Surrey, saw two bulls fighting in the meadow below his castle and a bull ran into the town, and that he enjoyed the chase so much that he gave the meadow to the butchers on condition that they provide a bull each year. Lying on the Great North Road, Stamford hosted several Parliaments in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In the 19th century the East Coast Main Line, which might have passed through the town, was routed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> after resistance, and Peterborough grew at Stamford's expense.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Stamford was the first conservation area designated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, under the Civic Amenities Act 1967, and more than 600 listed buildings stand in and around it. It has 17th- and 18th-century stone buildings, older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed ones and five medieval parish churches. St Leonard's Priory is the oldest building, legendarily founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>, and keeps Norman pillars and arches of about 1090 and a west front of about 1150. The Stamford Mercury has been published since 1712. The town had 19,701 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stamford, Lincolnshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aethelflaed of Mercia and Edward the Elder took Derby, Leicester, Nottingham and <b>Stamford</b> in 917 and 918.</blockquote><a href="/state/five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw">Five Boroughs of the Danelaw</a></li></ul></section>
